  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nAfternoon radar imagery shows scattered showers and thunderstorms \nclipping the southern tip of Menominee County but otherwise \nremaining south of the state line today as the first in a pair of \ndisturbances passes south through Wisconsin and Lower Michigan. \nElsewhere, visible satellite shows mostly clear skies over Lake \nSuperior and the western UP, with temperatures mostly in the 70s \nacross the area as of mid afternoon. The aforementioned secondary \ndisturbance is currently noted on water vapor imagery over eastern \nNorth Dakota and will approach the western UP as it tracks southeast \ninto the region late tonight. Models still display quite a bit of \nuncertainty with regards to timing and placement of this cluster of \nthunderstorms, with some CAMS keeping activity almost entirely south \nof the state line while a few others bring thunderstorms into the \nwestern and central UP overnight. If the more northern solution were \nto come to fruition, can't rule out that a few of these storms could \ninitially be on the stronger side as latest HREF depicts up to 1000 \nJ/kg MUCAPE and bulk shear around 40 kt. That said, activity would \nlikely weaken quickly with eastward extent as instability wanes and \nthunderstorms become elevated with any stronger winds struggling to \nreach the surface. SPC thus continues to keep the Day 1 marginal \nrisk confined to areas south of the state line through Friday \nmorning, but will continue to monitor how this thunderstorm threat \nevolves through the evening based on model and observational trends. \nThe quick hitting nature of the system will also tend to limit \nprecipitation amounts, but both NBM and HREF still depict a 50+ \npercent chance for a wetting rain of a quarter inch over the far \nwest and along the WI border through Friday morning.\n\nAny overnight precipitation should depart the UP by mid Friday \nmorning, leaving mostly cloudy skies and another day of temperatures \nin the 70s. Guidance continues to depict a few showers being \npossible over the western half in the afternoon, but would not \nexpect these to be particularly impactful with a less than 15% \nchance of thunderstorms and minimal QPF. Broad upper level ridging \nover the central CONUS continues to dominate the pattern through the \nholiday weekend. As such, temperatures will remain above normal into \nnext week with daytime highs residing in the mid to upper 70s to \nperhaps the low 80s in a few locations each day. The forecast looks \nto be mostly on the dry side through the weekend, but precipitation \nchances increase again early to mid next week as ridging starts to \nflatten out and makes way for a potentially stronger system to pass \nthrough the region. Details are still sparse, but NBM depicts a 50-\n70% chance for at least a quarter inch of rain across the UP by \nearly Wednesday, and also depicts a 40-70% chance for wind gust of \n40 mph or greater over a wide swath of Lake Superior and the \nKeweenaw in the same time frame. Will continue to monitor this \npotential as details hopefully start to come into better focus in \nthe coming days.",
